# Netty frame-limit encoder-direction correction — 2026-10-15T11:47:59Z

- Rechecked the pinned Java 5.5.1 `NettyEncoder.encode` source after the first
  frame-limit mapping. Java's `frameMaxLength` is read only by
  `NettyDecoder`; the encoder writes the frame without consulting that limit.
  The prior Rust change had reused the decoder limit for outbound request and
  response encoding, which would reject messages Java still sends.
- Rust now applies the configured property to inbound frame decoding only.
  Outbound encoding allows sizes above the decoder limit and remains bounded
  by the signed Java length-prefix representation.
- Extended the Java probe to encode a 4096-byte body under a configured
  decoder limit of 4096 and verify the resulting frame is larger than that
  limit. The Rust child-process loopback sends an inbound length prefix whose
  total frame exceeds the configured limit, verifies the connection closes,
  and verifies an outbound frame of that size still encodes.
- PASS: `cargo test -p rocketmq-classic-remoting --locked` (84 unit, 6 codec,
  1 fuzz regression, 5 security, and 86 transport tests); strict all-target,
  all-feature Remoting Clippy; Maven verify (37 scenarios, default/configured
  frame-limit probes including the encoder case, 2 base cross-decodes, 54 typed
  headers, and 14 typed bodies); format and diff checks.
- Code commit: `a06853ba fix(remoting): apply frame cap to inbound decode only`.
- Updated the Phase 2 compatibility row and release checklist. The reverse
  scan also recorded the public `codec::frame` constant and caller-selected
  limit functions as an unresolved P18-V01 API-disposition candidate.
- The complete P18-V02 workspace/server-free gate must be rerun after this code
  commit. Broad P18-V01, current-revision GitHub Ubuntu/P18-V04 evidence,
  Phase 19 local Docker/Broker automation, and release version/registry checks
  remain open.
- The pinned Java source repository was read-only. No plan, push, tag,
  publication, or Broker validation was performed.
